// AUTO-GENERATED by gen:manual-index. Do not edit by hand.
import type { ChapterText } from "@/components/manual/chapters";

export const MANUAL_INDEX: ChapterText[] = [
	{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Map Making App is a local-first desktop editor for building GeoGuessr maps. It is an alternative to map-making.app . It keeps the original's editing model and UI largely intact, but overhauls many systems to improve ergonomics, performance, or user experience. What's different? No account, no server. Every map lives on your computer. Built for large maps. Maps with millions of locations open and edit smoothly. Commit-based saving. Instead of a single \"save\", Map Making App records commits with diffs and a browsable version history you can revert to. Editor-state restore. Uncommitted changes are kept automatically, so you can close a map and pick up where you left off. Composable selections. Build selections from rules and combine them with intersection, union, and invert. Filters. Select by metadata: country, image date, camera type, or any field. Bulk operations. Apply one change to a whole selection at once, such as setting a field, pinning to a pano, or running enrichment. Metadata enrichment. Pull altitude, country, camera type, and capture dates onto your locations. Plugin system. Extend the editor with extra panels and tools, including a built-in coverage generator, plus a marketplace of downloadable ones. Seen history. A record of every panorama you have viewed, across all maps. Remote sync. Keep a local map in step with one on map-making.app or GeoGuessr. Power-user touches. Rebindable hotkeys, a command palette, and custom CSS. Terms Location. One Street View spot on a map: coordinates, a point of view (heading, pitch, zoom), an optional pinned panorama, tags, and metadata. Tag. A colored on/off label you attach to locations to categorize them. Selection. A named subset of locations, drawn on the map in its own color, built from rules you can combine. Commit. A saved version of the whole map. Commits chain together into a version history. Panorama. A single Street View image sphere. Each location resolves to one pano."},
	{"id":"first-run","title":"Getting started","text":"Map Making App stores everything in a single application data folder. You never have to manage files by hand, but it helps to know where they are. The data folder Windows: %APPDATA%\\app.map-making.local Linux: ~/.local/share/app.map-making.local macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/app.map-making.local Open it directly from Settings → Application → Data → Open data folder , or move it elsewhere with Change folder . It holds your maps, tags, version history, the seen history, logs, and any installed plugins. This is a work in progress and data loss can rarely happen. Keep backups. The recommended backup is the Export (all maps as a ZIP) button on the home screen. See Bulk backup & restore . Logs A unified log file ( mma.log ) is written inside the data folder under logs/ . It is the first thing to grab when reporting a bug. Open it directly from Settings → Advanced → Debug → Open log file ."},
	{"id":"map-list","title":"Map list/home screen","text":"When no map is open you see the home screen. The left column, Your Maps , lists every map with a header showing the total map and location counts. The right column holds a What's new panel. The corners hold the rest: Discord and the Manual bottom-left, bulk import/export, plugins, and settings bottom-right. What's new The right column shows a scrollable release history pulled from the changelog. Each version section lists its user-facing changes. Versions newer than your installed app are visually marked so you can see what an update would bring. The home screen: searchable, sortable map list with folders. The search / create bar Search maps. Type to filter the list by map name or label as you type. Terms are AND'd together, and label:name (or label:\"two words\" ) restricts a term to labels only. Clicking a label chip on a row toggles that term in and out of the search. Press Esc to clear. Sort. Order maps by Name , Last opened , Date created , or Location count . New folder. Type a name, then press the folder button to create a folder. New map. Type a name, then press the + button to create a map. If the search box has text and nothing matches, pressing Enter creates a map with that name and opens it. Map rows Open. Click a map's name to open it in its own editor window. The window title shows the map name. Drag handle. Drag a map by its handle onto a folder to move it; drag it to the root to move it out. Edit. The pencil renames the map and edits its labels (free-text keywords shown on the row and searchable). Delete. The trash icon deletes the map after a confirmation. You can also delete a map from inside its editor via the map rename dialog. Row fields. Each row can show location count, last opened, and date created. Choose which appear in Settings → Application → Map list . Folders Folders are collapsible and show their map and location totals. Deleting a folder moves its maps back to the root rather than deleting them."},
	{"id":"importing-existing","title":"Importing your existing maps","text":"If you already use map-making.app , you can bring your maps across in bulk. On the web app, use its Download data option to get a ZIP of all your maps. Then in Map Making App, use the Import icon in the bottom-right corner of the home screen. The bulk import flow Click the Import icon (bottom-right of the home screen). Choose a ZIP (a whole archive) or a single JSON file. Map Making App scans it and opens the Import Maps preview, listing each map with its location count, tag count, and folder. Pick which maps to bring in with All , None , or New only , or tick individual rows. Maps that match an existing map by name and location count are flagged duplicate and deselected by default. Expand the warnings section if any appear, then press Import N maps . A progress indicator runs while maps import. The neighbouring Export icon writes a single ZIP of every map, which is also the recommended backup. See Bulk backup & restore ."},
	{"id":"editor-layout","title":"Editor layout & the map","text":"Opening a map launches the editor: a resizable split between the map and the sidebar . Drag the divider between them to rebalance the panes (its position is remembered). The editor: map on one side, sidebar with Tags and Selections. The sidebar With no location open, the sidebar shows two sections: Tags. Create, color, and assign labels. A filter input and sort buttons (default, name, amount) sit at the top. Selections. Pick subsets of locations and combine them. Its header bar includes the plugin toolbar and a Commands... button that opens the command palette ( Ctrl + K ). Below the active selections is a pinnable toolbar holding shortcut buttons for commands - right-click it to add, remove, or reorder them. Some of them (find duplicates, filter by metadata, the random and spaced pickers, top/bottom K) open their form inline in the toolbar rather than a dialog. Clicking a location's marker swaps the sidebar for the location editor (covered later). The arrow at the top-left of the header goes back to the map list, as does Ctrl + Shift + W . Fullscreen map Ctrl + \\ hides the sidebar and gives the map the whole window. The location editor becomes a small floating preview in the corner instead of disappearing, so you can keep editing. What stays visible is configurable in Settings → Map → Fullscreen : the bottom bar and the mini location preview can each be turned off. Fullscreen map and fullscreen panorama ( F ) are mutually exclusive - entering one suspends the other and leaving it restores what you had. Basemap and layers Click the basemap control (top-left of the map) to choose the base imagery and open the layers panel: Basemap. Map , Satellite , OSM (OpenStreetMap), or Vector (keyless MapLibre styles). Layers. Terrain , Labels , and Panoramas (individual Street View dots, requires close zoom). The base Street View layer is always on. Street View. Coverage lines: Show lines filtered to Official , Unofficial , or All ; a color swatch for the lines; Make the lines thinner ; and Use blobby layer while zoomed out . Settings. Emphasise country borders , Emphasise subdivision borders , and switches to hide road labels , points of interest , transit , and highways . Map style. Default , Legacy (the classic GeoGuessr style), Muted , Midnight , Minimal , Vintage , Grayscale , Blueprint , Arctic , Ember , Forest , Noir , Dusk , any custom styles you add, and a Manage map styles link. The Vector basemap has its own style list (Liberty, Bright, Positron, Fiord (dark), Dark Matter, Voyager, Eclipse (dark), Colorful) and does not take the Google styling options above. Select-only mode Toggle Select-only mode ( O ) from the Map settings dropdown or its hotkey. When active, clicking the map never creates a new location; only existing markers are clickable. Useful when you want to browse and select without accidentally adding pins. Navigating the map Pan / zoom. Mouse drag and wheel, or the keyboard: W A S D to pan and Shift + W / Shift + S to zoom. Hold Alt for slow, fine movement. Shift + B fits the map to all locations, Shift + E to the selected ones, and Shift + 0 zooms all the way out. All of these are rebindable. Place search. The search control geocodes a place name via Nominatim and fits the map to it. Opacity slider. A slider in the top-right controls the opacity of the Street View coverage layer or the markers - click the icon next to it to switch between the two. P and M toggle each layer straight to hidden and back."},
	{"id":"bottom-bar","title":"The bottom bar","text":"The bar along the bottom holds the core map actions and status. The bottom bar: location count, Commit with diff, undo/redo, Seen, History, Import, Export. Location count. The total number of locations on the map. Commit. Saves the current working changes as a version. The button is disabled when there is nothing to commit. When there are pending changes, a diff appears beside it: +added , -removed , ±modified . Undo / Redo . Step backward and forward through your edits ( Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Y ). Seen. Opens your pano visit history. See Seen history . History. Opens the commit history for this map. See Version history & reverting . Import file. Brings locations into the current map. See Importing into a map . Export. Writes the map (or selection) out. See Exporting a map ."},
	{"id":"map-context-menu","title":"Map context menu","text":"Right-clicking the map opens a context menu. Right-clicking a location adds a section of actions for that location at the top, above the tools below. On a location Copy Street View link. Copies a Google Maps link aimed at that location's saved heading, pitch and zoom, with its tags carried on the URL. Shortened when Google's shortener answers. Copy pano ID. Copies the location's panorama ID. Download panorama. Stitches and saves the full panorama image. Copy to map... Copies the location into another map, picked by name. Duplicate location. Adds a copy at the same spot. Delete location. Anywhere on the map Start measurement / End measurement. Begins a distance measurement anchored at the clicked point, or ends an in-progress measurement. See Scoring, measurement & 5K radius . Copy coordinates. Copies the clicked point as lat, lng to the clipboard. Select this country / Select this subdivision. Selects everything inside the border containing the clicked point. Subdivisions need a Subdivision data set chosen in Settings → Map → Borders ; higher border detail downloads on first use. Delete this polygon. Removes the polygon selection(s) covering the clicked point. When several overlap there, the item says how many it will remove. Set latitude/longitude anchors. Draws a crosshair of latitude and longitude reference lines through the clicked point, useful for aligning locations. Clear latitude/longitude anchors. Removes those reference lines."},
	{"id":"adding-locations","title":"Adding locations","text":"Click the map Click an empty spot and Map Making App does not blindly drop a pin. It looks up the nearest Street View coverage, snaps to it, and creates the location at the corrected coordinates with the matching panorama. The candidate it keeps follows your per-map rules (official vs unofficial, camera quality, direction). See Loading mode & flags for what gets pinned. Crosshair at center Hold Enter to show a crosshair at the map center, then release to create a location there. This only works with no location open, since Enter saves the one you are editing. It can be a bit janky, but is supported if you want to go mouseless. Paste coordinates or URLs Paste a Google Maps URL to drop a location at that point. Short links ( maps.app.goo.gl ) are resolved automatically. Paste multiple URLs or coordinates (one per line) to import them all at once. A staged preview appears before they are added (see Importing into a map ). Paste into the search box. Pasting a URL or coordinate pair into the place search control also imports the location. The Min search radius per-map setting controls how far the snap looks for coverage - anywhere from 10m to 500m, defaulting to 50m. In particularly dense areas, decreasing it can help find the exact pano you are clicking at."},
	{"id":"location-editor","title":"Location editor & pano viewer","text":"Having a marker selected opens the location editor, which fills the sidebar with a live Street View panorama for that location. The location editor: live panorama, date picker, Save/Close/Delete, and the tag bar. Framing and saving Pan and look around to frame the view you want, then press Save (or Enter ). Save captures the current heading, pitch, zoom, panorama, and the location's coordinates, plus any tag changes. Save. Stores the framing and closes (or, in review mode, advances to the next location). Close. Closes without saving ( Esc ). Delete. Removes the location ( Delete ). Panned vs unpanned A location is unpanned until you have framed and saved a point of view; its heading is effectively zero. Saving a view makes it panned . The Unpanned selection finds everything still needing a view, which is useful by way of the \"review\" feature. Country and address The editor shows a flag and an address for the current pano, geocoded from its coordinates. Pick the provider in Settings → Editing → Geocoding : Local resolves the country offline with no requests, Nominatim gives a full street address, and Google reads what the panorama itself reports."},
	{"id":"pano-controls","title":"Pano controls & movement","text":"Overlay controls sit on top of the panorama. Which ones appear is up to you: Settings → Street View → Viewer controls has a switch per control. The panorama overlay: compass, zoom, return-to-spawn, jump, coordinates, and map links. Orientation and movement Compass. A wind rose that rotates with your heading; click it to point north, or Ctrl -click to cycle through linked panoramas at the same location. An optional heading tape is also available. Point north with N , spin 180 with T , and face along the nearest road with B . Zoom. Zoom the panorama in and out ( + / - ). Press 0 to zoom all the way out. Return to spawn. Snaps the pano back to the location's saved coordinates and clears any per-location pano pin ( R ). Shift + R just reloads the current panorama. Jump forward / backward. Hops roughly 100m along the road ( } / { ). Follow road. Walks along linked panos in the direction you face and adds each one as a new location ( G ). Look and walk from the keyboard. Arrow keys look around; Shift + Up / Shift + Down walk forward and back. Reference and links Altitude / zoom display. Shows the current pano's altitude (when available) and zoom level. Pano metadata. An optional corner readout of whether the location is pinned to a pano, plus every metadata field as a save would write it - it follows the pano you are viewing, not just what is stored. Handy while enriching or auditing. Map links. Open in Google Maps and copy a Street View link ( Ctrl + C ). Hold Shift while copying to omit tags from the URL, or Alt to copy the full long URL instead of a short link. Download panorama. Stitches and saves the whole panorama image ( Ctrl + Shift + S ). This is the full sphere, not what you are looking at. Screenshot. Captures the current view as a 1920x1080 PNG and copies it to the clipboard, ready to paste elsewhere. Hold Shift to save it as a file instead. Pan map to location. L centers the map on whatever you are looking at, which is useful once you have walked away from the marker. Display options Fullscreen. Expands the panorama and shows a minimap and a tag bar (each toggleable) ( F ). The minimap mirrors the editor map, rendering all markers, selections, and polygons, and follows the active location. The date picker, review bar, and geocoding info can each be shown in fullscreen too, from Settings → Street View → Fullscreen . Hide car. Masks the capture vehicle ( Ctrl + H ). Crosshair. A center crosshair for precise framing ( X ). Toggle pano UI. Hides the overlay controls for a clean view ( H ). By default this also hides the navigation arrows and click-to-go markers; turn that off in Settings → Street View → Navigation . Preview aspect ratio. The panorama pane can be shaped to a fixed aspect ratio (4:3, 16:10, 16:9, 21:9, 32:9) to match how the map will be played, or left Free to fill the pane. Set it in Settings → Street View . Lock viewport direction. V keeps your framing fixed relative to each panorama's own camera as you move between them, rather than snapping to each one's default view. Movement modes and speed Movement mode. The default is set in Settings: Moving (walk and look freely), No Move (look only, no walking), or NMPZ (no move, pan, or zoom). Shift + M cycles through the three. Look & pan speed. Pano look speed is adjustable in Settings → Street View , and map pan speed in Settings → Map → Navigation . Hold Alt for the slow modifier (its divisor is configurable in the same place)."},
	{"id":"coverage-dates","title":"Coverage dates & camera types","text":"Many places have several Street View captures over time. The date picker in the location editor lists every available capture date for the current spot. Picking a date Date picker. Choose any available capture to load that specific coverage. Cycle through dates with [ and ] . Camera badges. Each date can show a badge for its camera generation. Toggle badges in Settings → Street View → Date picker . For more information on camera generations, see this guide . Pinning a date Choosing a specific date pins that pano to the location (it sets Load as pano ID , see the next chapter). Use Return to spawn to clear the pin and fall back to the default coverage at the coordinates."},
	{"id":"loading-flags","title":"Loading mode & flags","text":"Load by coordinates vs by pano ID A location can resolve its panorama in one of two ways: By coordinates. The editor finds whatever the default coverage is at that point. This is the lighter option and is correct when the default coverage is the one you want. By pano ID (Load as pano ID). The location is locked to a specific panorama. Required whenever you want a particular date, an unofficial pano, or any coverage that is not what the coordinates alone would resolve to. Map Making App sets Load as pano ID automatically when you pick a specific date, or when it detects that the chosen pano is not the default at those coordinates. The per-map Use Pano ID locations by default setting forces it on for newly created locations. The Pano ID locations and Coordinate locations selections let you find locations by how they load, which is handy for auditing a map before export."},
	{"id":"reviewing","title":"Reviewing, duplicating & deleting","text":"Review mode Review mode walks you through a set of locations one at a time in the panorama viewer, tracking which ones you have already looked at. Start it from a selection's menu ( Review selection ), from the duplicate finder, or with Review selected locations from the command palette (also a default button in the pinned toolbar). Next / Previous. Ctrl + Right and Ctrl + Left . Save also advances to the next location and marks the current one reviewed. Delete. Removes the current location and moves on. Exit review. The X in the review header leaves review mode without discarding your progress. The header shows your position ( 3 / 50 ) and how many you have reviewed so far. Clicking another location's marker while a review is active jumps the cursor there if it is in the worklist; clicking off-list is a harmless peek that leaves your place untouched. Resuming a review Reviews are saved sessions, so you can stop partway and come back later. Re-running Review selection on the same selection resumes its in-progress session rather than starting over. Review sessions opens the session list. The review sessions dialog: in-progress and completed reviews with progress bars. In progress / Completed. Two tabs split active sessions from finished ones. Resume. Re-enters an in-progress session at the location you left off. Select reviewed / Select unreviewed. The two circle buttons turn a session's reviewed (or not-yet-reviewed) locations into a selection you can act on, without re-entering review mode. Delete. Discards the session. Select reviewed locations in the command palette is the broader version of those buttons: it selects everything you have ever marked reviewed on this map, pooled across every session rather than one. While a review is active, two overlay selections mirror its progress on the map: reviewed and unreviewed , so you can see at a glance what is left. Duplicating a location Press C in the location editor to duplicate the current location, then reframe the copy. Useful for capturing the same spot from multiple angles. Several locations at one spot Clicking a marker that has other locations within two meters of it does not open the panorama viewer. Instead the sidebar shows a same location panel listing everything at that spot, each with a thumbnail and its tags, because otherwise you would have no way to tell which of the stacked markers you just clicked. Click a thumbnail to open that particular location. Delete on a row removes just that one. Tick rows, then Keep selected (deletes the others) or Delete selected (deletes the ticked ones). Cancel leaves everything alone. This is the intended way out of accidental double-adds at a single pano. Cleaning up duplicates across the map Two different tools, easy to confuse: Merge duplicates... (command palette) works on the whole map. You give it a distance, and it collapses each cluster into one surviving location, combining the tags from all of them onto the survivor. One undoable edit. Prune duplicates (a Duplicates selection's row menu) discards the extras instead of merging them, and only touches that selection. See Selections . Reach for Merge when the duplicates carry tags you want to keep, and Prune when you just want them gone. Deleting Single. The Delete button or Delete in the editor. In bulk. Select locations, then press Delete on the map, or run Delete selected locations from the command palette or the pinned toolbar."},
	{"id":"tags","title":"Tags","text":"Tags are colored on/off labels you attach to locations. They are boolean: a location either carries a tag or it does not. Creating and assigning Create a tag from the Tags section; it gets a name and a color. Assign tags while editing a location, using the tag bar at the bottom of the editor (type to add, click suggestions, X to remove). Quicktags. While editing a location, number keys 1 through 9 toggle the first nine visible tags by position. These keys are rebindable. Quicktags and autocomplete suggestions follow your chosen tag sort order (name, count, or manual). Per-map hotkeys. You can assign a dedicated hotkey to any tag from its edit dialog. Pressing the key while a location is open applies or removes the tag. These bindings are stored per-map, not globally. See Controls & hotkeys . Click a tag pill in the Tags section to select every location carrying it; shift-click to extend a range. Colors, order, and visibility Color. Each tag has a color used for its markers, set with a color picker. Order & sort. Drag tags to reorder, or sort by name or count. Visibility. Hide a tag to declutter the map. A tag automatically becomes hidden when no location carries it. Suggestion limit. Cap how many tags appear in autocomplete and quicktag lists in Settings → Editing → Tags → Suggestions shown . Spacing. Tag gap controls how tightly tag pills pack together, useful on maps with hundreds of tags. Tree grouping With View mode set to tree ( Settings → Editing → Tags ), tags whose names contain / are grouped hierarchically, e.g. Europe/France nests under Europe . Parents show partial-selection indicators and inherit color. Two options refine it: Folder color (inherit from the first child, or a fixed color) and Truncate tag names to shortest unique path , which drops the redundant prefix from deeply nested names. Virtual folders. Create folder nodes that don't correspond to a real tag. They get their own color and can be renamed or recolored from an edit dialog, with an option to cascade changes to all descendant tags. Tag aliases. Place a tag at a second folder path via the Add alias item in a tag's context menu. The alias appears in the tree at the chosen path alongside the original. Tag tools Apply metadata as tags. Pick a metadata field and Map Making App creates one tag per distinct value, assigning each location the tag for its value (command palette). For example, applying countryCode turns a map into per-country tags in one step. Date and month fields offer a grouping choice (year, year-month, exact day, month of year, or hour of day), with an optional Location timezone checkbox; number fields can be bucketed by a fixed width (e.g. altitude in 500m bands). The same partition options are available in the Gradient plugin. Find and replace in tag names. Bulk-rename across tags (command palette). Download tag counts as CSV. Export per-tag counts (command palette). Delete selected tags. Removes every tag you currently have selected, and its assignments (command palette). Assign document links. Point tags at headings in a Google Doc, so the relevant section shows up beside the map. See Doclinks ."},
	{"id":"selections","title":"Selections","text":"A selection is a named subset of your locations, drawn on the map in its own color. The Selections section lists active selections with their match counts. Selections re-evaluate against the live map, so they stay correct as you edit. The Selections section: each rule drawn in its own color, with a per-selection menu. Selection types Tag and Untagged. Locations carrying a given tag, or carrying none. Unpanned. Locations not yet framed and saved. Pano ID locations / Coordinate locations. By how they load (pinned to a pano vs resolved by coordinates). Uncommitted. Locations added or changed since the last commit. Polygon. An area you draw as a polygon, rectangle, or freehand shape. Country / Subdivision. Hold Q and click the map to select a country. Hold Q + Shift and click to select an admin-1 subdivision (state, province, etc.). Enable subdivisions and choose their detail level in Settings → Map → Borders → Subdivision data . Duplicates. Locations within a chosen distance of each other. Validation state. Locations classified by the validator (see Validation ). Reviewed / Unreviewed. The two halves of a review session (see Reviewing, duplicating & deleting ). Filter. A query over metadata fields (see Filters ). Top / Bottom K. The highest or lowest K locations by a chosen field. Manual. Individual locations you toggle by Ctrl -clicking markers. Everything. All locations ( Ctrl + A ). Ctrl + D clears every selection. GeoJSON shapes. Load shapes from GeoJSON as selection turns the shapes in a .geojson file into polygon selections, which is how you bring boundaries in from other tools. Acting on a selection The menu on each selection row (the dots button) offers: Invert selection. Everything except this selection. Edit filter. Re-open the filter form (Filter selections only). Review selection. Step through its locations in the panorama viewer. Save as tag. Snapshot just this selection into a new tag, without disturbing the rest of your selection setup. Prune duplicates. On a Duplicates selection, thins each cluster out. Under 25m it keeps the single most relevant location per cluster; above that it thins to enforce that spacing. A location tagged keep pano is scored higher, so tag the ones you want kept before running it. Change color. Recolor the selection (any non-Tag selection; Tag selections use the tag's color). Download GeoJSON and Rename. For polygon selections. Deselect. Remove the selection. Each row also has an inline ghost button, which dims that selection on the map without removing it. Alt -click it to isolate that selection (ghost every other one); Alt -click again to restore all. Polygons can be removed straight off the map by holding E and clicking the one you want gone. Delete selected locations also lives in the command palette. To tag the whole selection at once, use the Bulk-add tag input in the pinned toolbar - type to filter matching tags, or press Enter to create a new tag by name. Picking a random subset Pick random locations from selection (command palette) takes a count and replaces the current selection with that many locations chosen at random from it. If you ask for more than the selection holds, it is clamped to the whole selection. Handy for sampling a large map down to a manageable set to review or export. Reducing density Pick evenly spaced locations from selection (command palette) replaces the current selection with a spread-out subset of itself. Choose Count to keep a target number of locations, spacing them as far apart as possible, or Min distance to keep as many locations as fit while staying at least that many meters apart. Use this to thin a dense cluster of locations without leaving obvious gaps or clumps. Picking from each selection Both pickers offer from each of N selections whenever two or more selections are active. The count then becomes a per-selection cap rather than a total: with eight tags selected and a count of 25, you get up to 25 locations from each tag, unioned into one selection. A location carried by two of them is only picked once, so the total can come out below count x N. This is how you build an evenly weighted map out of tags with wildly different location counts. It works for any kind of selection, not just tags - per polygon, per filter, or per bucket of a gradient."},
	{"id":"selection-algebra","title":"Selection algebra","text":"Selections are composable. You can combine and negate them to express complex queries without writing any code. Combining selections: intersection (AND), union (OR), and invert (NOT). Operations Intersection (AND). Locations matching every one of the combined selections. Command: Intersect (AND) selections . Union (OR). Locations matching any of them. Command: Union (OR) selections . Invert (NOT). Everything except a selection. Command: Invert selection , or per-row from the selection menu. Drag to combine Drag one selection row onto another to combine them directly in the sidebar, building nested expressions whose children are shown indented under the result. Example To find locations tagged France and inside a polygon you drew, but not already panned: add the Tag, Polygon, and Unpanned selections, intersect Tag with Polygon, then intersect that with the inverted Unpanned."},
	{"id":"filters","title":"Filters","text":"A Filter selection queries a field on each location and selects the matches. Build one with Filter by metadata... (command palette, or its button in the pinned toolbar, where the form opens inline), or edit an existing one from its selection menu. Fields you can query Built-in fields. Latitude and Longitude , ID , Created and Modified timestamps, Pano ID , Load as pano ID , Tag count , Heading , Pitch , and Zoom . Metadata fields. Any field present on the map, such as countryCode , cameraType , panoType , altitude , imageDate , exact date, timezone, and any custom fields you have added. Custom fields can be typed as text, number, date, month, enum, or array. Operators = / != equals and not equals. > / < / >= / <= numeric and date comparisons. between a range, with between (any year) and between (any date) variants for date and month fields, so you can match, for example, \"any year, June to August\". has / does not have presence of a value. contains / does not contain for array fields, plus length-based comparisons (length =, length >, length between, etc.). Enum fields (such as camera type) offer only the equality operators and a value list. Date and month fields use a date picker, including any-year mode for season-style filters. A date pick at midnight means \"the whole day\" - the filter spans the full calendar day. Use the clear-time button on the picker to reset to day-grain. Timezone Date filters compare against your local timezone by default. Check Location timezone on the filter to compare in each location's own timezone instead, which is important when filtering by time of day across many countries. Stepping through periods Filter selection rows for date and number ranges show left/right arrow buttons. These shift the filter window forward or backward by its own span, so you can step through dates day-by-day or numbers band-by-band. Between the min and max inputs, copy buttons let you duplicate one bound to the other. Filters operate on stored metadata. Run enrichment first so the fields you want to filter on exist. See Metadata enrichment ."},
	{"id":"saved-selections","title":"Saved selections","text":"Selection rules can be saved and reused across maps via the command palette. They are stored with your app settings, not tied to a single map. Save current selections... Names the active selection set and stores it. Apply saved selection... Re-applies a stored set to the current map. Portability Because saved selections move between maps, tag rules are stored by tag name (matched case-insensitively on apply). Selection types that cannot be made portable are excluded from saving: Manual , explicit ID lists, Validation state , and review progress. Polygons, filters, untagged/unpanned/pano-id rules, uncommitted, top/bottom K, duplicates, and composites all carry over. A rule that no longer applies to the map you apply it to - a tag name that doesn't exist there - is quietly dropped rather than failing the whole set."},
	{"id":"bulk-operations","title":"Bulk operations","text":"Bulk operations apply an action to many locations at once. Open them from the command palette ( Bulk Operations group) or the relevant tool. Each opens a runner where you choose a scope and watch progress. Scope Every bulk operation runs against either All locations or the Current selection . If you have an active selection, scope defaults to it. The operations Validate locations. Checks coverage and classifies problems. See Validation . Enrich metadata fields. Fetches metadata for the chosen fields. See Metadata enrichment . Set metadata field value. Writes a chosen value into a field across the scope. Besides custom metadata fields, the picker also offers the built-in camera fields Heading , Pitch , and Zoom , so you can set a fixed point of view across many locations at once. For numeric fields you can enter an expression instead of a constant, such as mod(heading + 180, 360) . Expressions can reference any field on the location and use functions like mod , clamp , abs , round , min , and max . Pan headings along road. Sets each location's heading to face along the road, choosing forwards or backwards . Clear metadata fields. Removes chosen fields from the scope. Pin locations to pano ID. Resolves and pins a specific panorama to each location (sets Load as pano ID ). Download panoramas. Stitches and saves the full panorama image for every location in the scope. Slow and large - scope it deliberately."},
	{"id":"managing-fields","title":"Managing metadata fields","text":"The Enrichment dialog (the database icon in the editor header, beside the map name) holds one table covering every metadata field known to the map: whether to enrich it, and how it is defined. What you can edit Enrich. Whether this field is fetched when enrichment runs. See Metadata enrichment . Field key. Rename a field. Renaming to an existing field's key merges them; on conflict you choose which field's values win. Both rename and merge report how many locations are affected and cannot be undone. Label. The display name shown in pickers and filters. Type. Text , Number , Date/time , Month (YYYY-MM) , Enum , or Array . Enum fields get a value editor where you list the allowed values and give each a display label; it can pre-fill the list from the values already on your locations. Compare as. How the field is compared during disambiguation: Auto , Numeric , Circular (with a wrap period such as 360 for degrees or 24 for hours), or Categorical . Delete. Removes the field and clears its values from every location. This cannot be undone. Each field row shows a coverage icon indicating what percentage of locations have a value for it. Edits apply immediately - there is no Save button - and the destructive ones ask first."},
	{"id":"autosave","title":"Autosave & editor-state restore","text":"Your working changes are persisted to disk automatically, shortly after you stop editing. Nothing you do is lost if you close the app. Autosave. Writes your uncommitted changes in the background. This is not a commit, it is a safety net. Editor-state restore. Reopen a map and your uncommitted changes are still there, exactly as you left them. Autosave keeps your edits safe, but it does not create a version. Use Commit to mark milestones you can return to. See the next chapter."},
	{"id":"commits","title":"Commits & diffs","text":"A Commit bakes the current working changes into a permanent version. It is the durable checkpoint, distinct from the always-on autosave. The Commit button. In the bottom bar. Disabled when there is nothing to commit ( Ctrl + S ). A plain click commits right away. Shift +click opens a dialog showing the pending diff and an optional message describing the change, which then labels that commit in the history. The pending diff. Beside the button: +added , -removed , and ±modified counts since the last commit. Undo/redo operates on individual edits within your session. Commits are the browsable, durable checkpoints. Use undo for \"oops\", use commits for milestones. Committing clears the undo/redo history. Browse past commits from the History button in the bottom bar (see Version history & reverting )."},
	{"id":"version-history","title":"Version history & reverting","text":"The History button opens the commit list for the map. Commits form a chain, each recording the change from the previous one. The commit table Columns. Date, a short hash, the commit message and change diff (+added / -removed / ~modified), and the resulting location count. View changes. Click a row with changes to enter a full-map diff view: added locations appear green, removed red, and modified amber, with a sidebar showing aggregate counts per category. Rows with no diff are not clickable. Revert / Restore. The newest commit shows a Revert button; older commits show Restore . Either creates a new commit that returns the map to that commit's state. The button asks for confirmation before it acts, so nothing happens on a single misclick. If the map has no commits yet, the dialog prompts you to press Commit to create the first. Commit writes immediately. To attach a message first, hold Shift while clicking Commit."},
	{"id":"enrichment","title":"Metadata enrichment","text":"Enrichment fetches extra information about each location's panorama and stores it on the location. This metadata can store non-boolean data (numbers, dates, strings), unlike tags, which makes it the foundation for filtering, sorting, and analysis. Available fields Five fields are on by default : Altitude. Elevation of the pano in meters. Country code. ISO country of the coverage. Camera type. The generation/quality: Gen 1, Gen 2/3, Gen 4, tripod, trekker, or bad cam. Pano type. Official, unknown, or user-uploaded. Image date. The year-month of capture. Six more are off by default and must be opted into: Exact date. The precise capture timestamp, resolved by binary-searching Google's servers. Much slower than other fields (multiple requests per location). Display format (date only vs date + time) and timezone (location-local vs UTC) are configurable in Settings → Street View → Date picker . Timezone. The pano's IANA timezone (e.g. America/New_York ). Pairs with exact date for time-of-day filtering in local time. Driving direction. Which side of the road the coverage car was driving on. Uploader. The name of the user or entity that uploaded the panorama. Coverage dates. The full list of capture dates available at that location across all time periods. Subdivision. The first-level administrative area (state, province, region) containing the location. Resolved offline against the subdivision border dataset (downloaded automatically on first use), so boundaries near borders may differ slightly from other sources. Pairs with Apply metadata as tags to tag a whole map by subdivision. Plugins can register additional enrichment fields. The Enrichment dialog Open it from the database icon in the editor header, beside the map name. It holds two things: Enrich locations. The per-map switch for saving metadata automatically as you browse. The field table. One row per field, with an Enrich checkbox controlling whether that field is fetched. Unticking a field skips it. The rest of each row defines the field itself - see Managing metadata fields . Both are per-map settings. Running enrichment Automatically on view. With Enrich locations on, each location is enriched as you open it in the viewer. Metadata is always fetched for display; this setting controls whether it gets saved to the location. Turn it off if you want to browse without modifying location data. In bulk. Run Enrich metadata fields from the command palette, choosing a scope. The runner skips locations that are already enriched unless you force a refresh. Slow fields (exact date) show their own progress. Using enriched data Once locations carry metadata, it unlocks several features: Filter by metadata. Build selections based on field values - for example, all locations from 2023, or all Gen 4 coverage above 500m altitude. See Filters . Apply metadata as tags. Pick a metadata field and Map Making App creates one tag per distinct value, assigning each location automatically. For example, applying countryCode tags your entire map by country in one step. Date fields offer grouping choices (year, year-month, day, month of year, hour of day). Number fields can be bucketed by a fixed width (e.g. altitude in 500m bands). This is in the command palette under Tags . See Tags . Gradient coloring. The Gradient plugin colors locations on a spectrum by any numeric or date field. See Built-in plugins ."},
	{"id":"doclinks","title":"Doclinks","text":"A doclink ties a tag to a heading in a Google Doc. Select that tag and the relevant section of the doc appears in a panel beside the map. It exists for the common case of a meta guide living in a doc while the map lives here: instead of alt-tabbing to find what Poland/Bollard means, you select the tag and read it in place. Assigning links Run Assign document links... from the command palette (Tags group). Paste the document's URL and Map Making App loads its outline: every heading, listed in order. Arm a tag by clicking it, then click headings to attach or detach that tag. One tag can point at several headings, and one heading can serve several tags. The tag pane mirrors the sidebar's tag list - same folders, order, and view mode, opening with the folders you had expanded. Both panes have filter fields, and when the map links more than one document, buttons under the URL switch between them. Match names proposes links wherever a heading's text equals a tag's name (ignoring case and accents; a nested tag like Poland/Bollard also matches a heading named just Bollard when no other tag shares that leaf). Review the suggestions, dismiss any you don't want, then Link all . The document has to be readable by anyone with the link - Map Making App fetches it as an anonymous reader, with no sign-in. Reading them Once any tag on the map carries a doclink, a document icon appears above the sidebar and opens the panel. From then on it follows your selections: select a doclinked tag and the panel shows its section. The panel header has: Refresh. Re-fetches the document, bypassing the cache. Use it after editing the doc. Whole document. Swaps the single-section view for the full document, scrolled to the linked heading. Pin. Stops the panel following selections, so it stays on the section you are reading. Open in browser. Opens the real document externally. When the selected tag points at more than one heading, numbered buttons below the header page between them. Drag the panel's inner edge to resize it. Google Maps links inside the document are live: clicking one selects that location if it is already on the map, and adds it if it is not. Doclinks travel with the map. Exporting with Save app data on carries them, and re-importing adopts them onto tags that have none. To repoint a document, clear its links first (the trash button in the assign dialog) and reimport."},
	{"id":"validation","title":"Validation","text":"Validation checks whether each location still has valid Street View coverage and classifies any problems. Run it via Validate locations in the command palette (a bulk operation with a scope). Problem categories Valid location. No issues. Newer coverage available. The pinned pano has a more recent version in the timeline. Coverage updated since last view. A coordinate-based location now resolves to a different, newer pano. Not found. No coverage at the coordinates or pano ID. Pano ID broke. The pinned pano ID no longer resolves. Unofficial. Resolves to user-uploaded coverage. Badcam, but good coverage available. The current coverage is a low-quality camera, but a better one exists in the timeline. Results become a Validation state selection you can act on, for example review every \"Pano ID broke\" location and re-pin it."},
	{"id":"seen","title":"Seen history","text":"When enabled, Map Making App records every panorama you actually look at, across all maps, into a \"seen\" history. It is useful for going back to locations you have previously viewed. Recording Recording is controlled in Settings → Editing → Seen . Turn Log viewed panos on or off, optionally Save thumbnails , and choose the thumbnail resolution (Low 160x90, Medium 320x180, High 640x360) to trade storage for clarity. Browsing The Seen button in the bottom bar opens the history with thumbnails, a flag, and the address or coordinates of each visit. Filter by: Country. A dropdown of countries seen. Map. A dropdown of maps. Address search. A text box that matches the stored address. The history is paginated and capped at the most recent 10,000 entries, pruning the oldest as new ones arrive. On the map Toggle seen locations overlay (command palette) draws every seen pano as a dot on the current map. Dots are colored by whether the pano is already on this map: blue means it is, and clicking opens that location; orange means it is only in history, and clicking previews the pano read-only with the option to add it. Nothing is added to the map until you say so. This is the fast way to see where you have already been while filling in a region - the orange dots are the panos you looked at and did not keep."},
	{"id":"scoring","title":"Scoring, measurement & 5K radius","text":"GeoGuessr scores a guess from the distance to the true location and the size of the playable area. A map's score bounds define that area, which sets how harshly distance is penalized. Edit them in the Scoring section of the map settings dropdown. The three modes Automatic based on locations. Derived from the bounding box of your locations and updated as you add or remove them. World map (ACW). The whole-world curve, like a worldwide map. Fixed bounds. An explicit box you enter as south, west, north, and east coordinates. Measuring distance Right-click the map and choose Start measurement to anchor a line at that point. Click to add the next point, or drag any point to adjust it. Map Making App also converts that distance into the GeoGuessr score it would earn under the current score bounds. Press Escape , or choose End measurement from the menu, to finish. The 5K radius (perfect-score circle) Enable Display 5K radius in the map settings dropdown to draw a score-decay circle around the location you have open - the area inside which a guess still scores 5000. The circle's radius is derived from the map's score bounds (at least 25m, but larger for world maps), and it follows you as you walk away from the marker. Score bounds and the 5K radius circle drawn around locations."},
	{"id":"settings-overview","title":"App settings vs per-map settings","text":"Map Making App has two distinct layers of settings. Knowing which is which avoids confusion. App settings, split into Street View, Map, Editing, Keyboard, Application, Integrations, and Advanced sections. App settings (global) Opened from the gear icon (bottom-right). These apply everywhere and are stored on your machine, not on the map. Sections are listed down the left; the search box above them filters every section at once, including shortcuts, which is usually faster than hunting through them. Street View. Navigation and movement defaults, which overlay controls are visible, what the fullscreen panorama shows, and the date picker. Map. Map pan speed and the Alt slow-down; the default, active, and staged marker colors; panorama dot color and sizing (constant on screen vs growing as you zoom in); default polygon color; the country/subdivision border datasets; and what the fullscreen map shows. Marker size is per-map, not here. Editing. Tag display and suggestions, the seen history, and your geocoding provider. Keyboard. Every rebindable shortcut. See Controls & hotkeys . Application. Session restore, which fields show on map rows, updates, and where your data folder lives. Integrations. Discord Rich Presence and the local REST API. Advanced. Custom CSS and debug tools. Fullscreen has two independent modes with their own settings: the fullscreen panorama (under Street View ) and the fullscreen map (under Map ). Per-map settings Opened from the Map settings dropdown above the map. These belong to the current map only: Selecting new locations. Point view along the road and a Direction preference (forwards, backwards, most northern/eastern/southern/western, or random); prefer official over unofficial; prefer higher quality over newer images; disallow unofficial; use Pano ID locations by default; and a Min search radius slider. Map behaviour. Show location previews on hover, and Select-only mode (map clicks never add locations). Scoring. The score bounds editor. Display. Marker style (Pin, Circle, Camera direction arrow), marker size, Display 5K radius , and Show click search radius at cursor . Enrichment is also per-map, but it lives in its own dialog rather than this dropdown. See Metadata enrichment ."},
	{"id":"controls-hotkeys","title":"Controls & hotkeys","text":"The Keyboard section of Settings holds every rebindable shortcut, grouped by where it applies: Commands, Global, Location Editor, Map Navigation, Review, and Quicktag. Every command in the palette can be given a key here, not just the built-in actions. Movement speeds live elsewhere: map pan speed and the Alt slow-down in the Map section, pano look speed in the Street View section. Rebinding keys Click to rebind. Click a shortcut's current binding, then press the new key combination. Press Backspace or Delete while recording to clear it, or Esc to cancel. Filter. A search box narrows the (long) shortcut list. Reset. Each customized binding shows a Reset button; there is also Reset all to defaults . Conflict resolution If you assign a combination already in use, Map Making App shows what it is bound to and offers to Reassign (which clears it from the other action). Conflicting rows are flagged with a warning you can click to jump to the other action. Some combinations are blocked: for example, a combination using Alt cannot shadow a navigation action, because Alt is the slow modifier. Per-map hotkeys Each map can have its own hotkeys that override global bindings while the map is open. Two kinds of per-map binding are available: Tag hotkeys. Assign a key to a tag from its edit dialog. Pressing the key while a location is open toggles the tag. Copy-to-map. Assign a key to another map. Pressing the key copies the active location into that map. Movement speeds Pan speed. How fast the map pans with the keyboard ( Settings → Map → Navigation ). Pan to imported locations. Whether the map jumps to newly imported locations ( Settings → Map → Navigation ). Pano look speed. How fast the panorama camera turns with the keyboard ( Settings → Street View ). Alt slow-down. The divisor applied while you hold Alt for fine control ( Settings → Map → Navigation )."},
	{"id":"data-folder","title":"Data folder & diagnostics","text":"Settings → Application → Data is where your files live. Open data folder. Opens the application data folder in the OS file browser. See Getting started for what is in it. Change folder. Moves your maps somewhere else, such as a larger drive or a synced location. You confirm the new path before anything moves, and Reset to default puts it back. Diagnostics Stats for nerds. An overlay ( Ctrl + Shift + D ) showing version, counts, storage size, and live render statistics. The first thing to check when something feels slow. FPS counter. A lighter always-on frame counter, in Settings → Advanced → Debug . Open log file. Also in Settings → Advanced → Debug . Grab this when reporting a bug. Custom CSS. Settings → Advanced takes a stylesheet that is applied over the whole app, so you can restyle anything you can select. Back up before anything risky (bulk deletes, large imports, moving the data folder). The recommended backup is Export on the home screen, which writes every map to a single ZIP. See Bulk backup & restore ."},
	{"id":"plugin-system","title":"Plugins","text":"Plugins extend Map Making App with new tools and panels. A plugin can add a sidebar panel, a full-screen modal , or a location panel embedded in the location editor, and it has full access to the app's data and Street View tools. Some add nothing visible at all and only register extra enrichment fields. The plugin marketplace: Core (built-in) and Additional (downloadable) tabs. The marketplace Open it from the puzzle-piece icon in the bottom-right corner. It has two tabs: Core. The plugins built into Map Making App. Each card has an Enable / Disable button. Additional. Downloadable plugins from the online registry, with Install , Enable / Disable , an uninstall (trash) button, and an Update action when a newer version is available. Cards carry small markers you can hover for an explanation: a flask for experimental plugins, and a wand for ones that only add data fields. A plugin that needs a newer version of Map Making App than you have says so instead of offering to install. Enabled plugins activate automatically whenever a map is open, and the enabled set is remembered between sessions."},
	{"id":"builtin-plugins","title":"Built-in plugins","text":"The Core tab includes: Map generator. Generates Street View locations from coverage inside regions you draw, with extensive quality filters (camera generation, date ranges, official vs unofficial, minimum spacing, and more). It can follow linked panoramas to spread coverage. A Sampling control chooses how candidate points are distributed: Random , Uniform (Poisson disk), Coverage (blue-line sampling), or Grow (kernel-based expansion). Additional options include searching within panorama descriptions, filtering by number of links, setting a fixed output zoom level, and adjusting the generation speed. Settings changes apply mid-run. An optional search-coverage overlay visualizes where the generator has searched. The generator session persists across view switches, so you can leave and return without losing progress. Vali. Generates locations from pre-built coverage data rather than by probing Street View, so it is much faster than the generator where that data exists. Progress streams as it runs. Coverage data is fetched automatically the first time a country is needed; the download button in the panel header does it on demand for a country, a continent, or everything at once, and can force a full redownload. When you open the panel it quietly checks whether the data you already downloaded has been superseded, marks the download button if so, and offers to update just those countries. Distribution. A live bar chart of how your locations are spread across countries, with flags and counts. It resolves countries offline from coordinates, so it works with zero enrichment; a Coordinates / Metadata toggle switches between coordinate-based counts and your enriched countryCode field when one is present. Disambiguate. Ranks metadata fields by how strongly they separate your active selections, helping you find what distinguishes one group from another. Gradient. Colors locations by a field value using gradient buckets. Supports the same date/number partition modes as Apply metadata as tags . A scope selector lets you color only the current selection, with bucket boundaries computed from the subset. Pivot Table. Cross-tabulates selections against location metadata. map-making.app sync and GeoGuessr. Link the open map to a remote map and keep the two in step. See below. The Map generator: draw regions, set coverage filters, and generate. The Distribution plugin: locations per country as a bar chart. Syncing with a remote map Both sync plugins work the same way. Sign in, search the remote account for a map, and Link this map to pair it with the one you have open. On the first sync you choose how the two sides are reconciled: Merge. Keep everything from both sides. Never deletes. Mirror from remote. Make the local map match the remote one. Mirror from local. Make the remote map match yours. After that it is a plain three-way merge: Sync now pushes your changes, pulls theirs, and lists anything it could not decide (both sides added, both sides edited, deleted on one side and edited on the other) for you to resolve. Sync continuously while this map is open does it in the background as you edit. Both are marked experimental - link a map you have a backup of before trusting them with real work."},
	{"id":"installing-plugins","title":"Installing user plugins","text":"From the marketplace On the Additional tab, press Install on a registry plugin. It downloads into your plugins folder, enables itself, and activates on the open map. Use the trash button to uninstall. Some plugins ship a sidecar : a helper program that does work the app can't (image models, OCR). Those are downloaded as part of installing the plugin, with a progress bar, and updated the same way. They are larger than the plugin itself, so the first install takes a while. Manually Plugins live in a plugins/ subfolder of your application data folder, one folder per plugin. To install one by hand: Copy the plugin's folder into your plugins directory. Restart the app. Enable it in the marketplace. Each plugin folder contains a manifest.json (its identity: id , name , description , icon , version , and main , the entry file) and the entry JavaScript file."},
	{"id":"importing-into-map","title":"Importing into a map","text":"The Import file button in the bottom bar brings locations into the current map. You can also drag a JSON or CSV file straight onto the editor window; a \"Drop file to import\" overlay appears while you hover. Either way an import panel opens in the sidebar showing a preview before anything is committed. Staged locations appear on the map in a distinct color (configurable in Settings → Map → Markers → Staged marker color ). The import panel Location count. How many locations the file contains. Tags in file. The tags detected in the file. Fields. Every metadata field found, each with its value count and a checkbox. Untick a field to drop it on import; your choices are remembered. Tag all imported locations. Apply one tag to everything you import. Warnings. Any problems detected during parsing. Formats and field names Map Making App auto-detects JSON vs CSV and tolerates common field-name variants (for example latitude/lat, longitude/lng/lon, pano/panoId), so files from various tools usually import without manual mapping. Press Import to commit, or Discard to cancel. Very large imports (500,000+ locations) auto-commit immediately and cannot be undone. A confirmation dialog warns you before this happens."},
	{"id":"exporting-map","title":"Exporting a map","text":"The Export button writes locations out. You choose the scope, a format, and options. Scope Export everything. The whole map. Export selection. Only the current selection (disabled when nothing is selected). Options File name. The output file name. Save zoom levels. Include each location's zoom. Save app data. Include app-specific data such as tags. Turning it off makes the file smaller, which helps when uploading maps with 100K+ locations to GeoGuessr. Tag order is preserved through export and re-import. Bypass GeoGuessr auto-panning for locations with 0 heading. Keeps your deliberately unpanned locations unpanned in the game. These three options apply to the JSON and GeoJSON formats. CSV ignores them. Formats JSON (recommended). Copy to clipboard or download. The only format that carries everything. CSV. Copy or download. Coordinates only - a lat,lng file. No heading, pitch, or zoom, no pano IDs, no tags, and no metadata, whatever the options above are set to. Use it to hand coordinates to another tool, not to move a map. GeoJSON. Download, for use in non-GeoGuessr mapping tools. Each point carries its tag names. Download opens a native Save as dialog so you choose exactly where the file lands, and a notification confirms when the export is written."},
	{"id":"bulk-backup","title":"Bulk backup & restore","text":"From the home screen, two icons in the bottom-right corner handle whole-library transfer. Export. Writes a single ZIP of every map (named with today's date). This is the recommended backup. Import. Restores from such a ZIP, or imports a single JSON, with the same preview and duplicate-detection flow described in Importing your existing maps . Make a backup before risky operations (bulk deletes, large imports, moving your data folder). Restoring is just a re-import of the ZIP. To backup your data otherwise: You can use Git on your app data folder (see Getting started ). You can point your data folder at a network location ( Settings → Application → Data → Change folder ). Individual maps can also be mirrored to map-making.app or GeoGuessr with the sync plugins, though those are meant for keeping a remote map current rather than as a backup. See Built-in plugins ."},
];
